Look up market limits and reject an orde.ts:
Read `market.limits.amount.min` and `market.limits.cost.min` for BTC/USDT on Binance, then validate a proposed order against them before sending it — this tiny order is rejected locally instead of by the exchange.

```typescript
import ccxt from 'ccxt';

const exchange = new ccxt.binance();
await exchange.loadMarkets();
const symbol = 'BTC/USDT';
const market = exchange.market(symbol);
const ticker = await exchange.fetchTicker(symbol);
const price = ticker.last ?? 0;
const amount = 0.00001;

const minAmount = market.limits.amount?.min ?? 0;
const minCost = market.limits.cost?.min ?? 0;
const cost = amount * price;
console.log(`Min amount: ${minAmount}, min cost: ${minCost}`);
console.log(`Order: ${amount} BTC @ ${price} = ${cost} USDT`);

if (amount < minAmount) {
    console.log(`Rejected: amount ${amount} is below the minimum ${minAmount}`);
} else if (cost < minCost) {
    console.log(`Rejected: cost ${cost} is below the minimum ${minCost}`);
} else {
    console.log('Order passes the market limits');
}
```

